# nix-direnv serves its cached layout until one of the files it watches is
# newer than the cache, and it reads that list when `use flake` runs: a watch
# declared after that line is not part of the comparison. On its own it
# watches only `flake.nix` and `flake.lock`, while everything substantive in
# this flake lives under `nix/` - so editing any of it would change the
# devshell without direnv noticing, and the shell would keep serving the
# previous environment, successfully, all session.
#
# So the files are named here, before `use flake`. A `watch_dir nix` would not
# do: a directory's own mtime moves only when an entry is added or removed,
# not when a file in it is edited.
watch_file nix/*.nix nix/*/*.nix

use flake;
